{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. Tests pass and the working tree has the fix plus `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Cause:** `similarity()` in `src/app.py:30` returns a Python `float`, which lands in `similar[].score` and is rejected by DynamoDB's serializer (`TypeError: Float types are not supported`). The first publish succeeds only because the table is empty — `related()` returns `[]`, so no float exists to crash on. From the second publish onward, every item carries computed float scores and `put_item` blows up, producing exactly the 500s in `logs/error.log`. I reproduced it locally (201, then 500) using the fake table before fixing.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `src/app.py:30` — the one-line boundary conversion: `Decimal(str(round(...)))`, so stored scores are clean `Decimal('0.35')`-style values instead of floats. I audited every value that feeds the item; the score was the only computed/float one.\n- `tests/test_publish.py` — two regression tests that exercise the populated path: one seeds a record, publishes a second, and asserts the score is a `Decimal`; the other publishes twice through `handler()` and expects 201 both times. The old test only ever covered the empty-table path, which is why this shipped.\n\n**Verification:** `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` → 3 tests, all OK; manual double-publish returns 201 twice.\n\n`ANSWER.md` documents the cause, the fix, and which parts of the colleague's note applied (the diagnosis, boundary-conversion, trace-computed-values, and seed-before-write test advice) versus which didn't (I used `Decimal` rather than their int-percentage approach to keep the documented 0–1 score scale, and used the repo's boto3-faithful fake table instead of a real one since the failure happens client-side during serialization)."}
